<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- Least privilege. Each entitlement and why Vauta needs it:

     app-sandbox: Vauta runs in the App Sandbox. Its encrypted store lives in
       the app container; nothing else on disk is reachable without the user.

     device.audio-input: the microphone, transcribed on device as "Me". Audio
       is never written to disk. System and meeting-app audio is captured with
       a Core Audio process tap, which needs no entitlement (macOS asks the
       user for permission).

     personal-information.calendars: optional and off by default. When the
       user turns it on, Vauta reads only the chosen calendars, about ten
       minutes around now, to name the meeting and its attendees. Nothing is
       cached. The same access is used, read-only, for the pre-meeting brief (the event about to
       start, and Reminders if the user turns them on). It is also used for the actions after a meeting, and
       only through one file of the app: when the user presses Add, Vauta adds the one reminder or event they
       confirmed (an event only when Calendar is already on; otherwise a file is handed to Calendar, which asks
       where to put it), reads the titles of the one list, or the one day of the one calendar, it adds to so as
       not to add it twice, and removes only what it created, when the user asks. It never edits, completes or
       deletes anything it did not create and never creates a list or a calendar. No entitlement was added;
       whether Reminders needs its own key under the sandbox is to be checked with a signed build.

     network.client: the ONLY destination is the model server the user chose
       in Settings, enforced in code (LLMServerConfig and LLMClient): loopback
       on this Mac, or HTTPS (TLS 1.3) to a private address on the local
       network, with the private CA as the only trust anchor and optional SPKI
       pins, no proxy and no redirects. Speech models are downloaded by the
       system, not by Vauta. There is no network.server: Vauta never listens.

     files.user-selected.read-write: only files the user picks in an open or
       save panel: a recording to import (read), the CA certificate of a
       network model server (read), the file an export or an encrypted backup
       is saved to (write) and a backup to restore (read). Vauta gets no other access to the user's files.

     files.bookmarks.app-scope: remembers the folders and files the user adds as document sources (Settings →
       Documents), read-only, so they keep working after a restart: Vauta stores an app-scoped, read-only
       security-scoped bookmark of each one in the encrypted store. Revocable: removing a source deletes its
       bookmark. Vauta never writes to a source. -->
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
